| 480 | in the one-library world the compiled checker's `case` without a comparison operator picks `=`, where the specification gives ∈, for a guard typed by a type variable bounded by a `Contains` or by a union of `Contains` types | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (checker) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/case.tex:47-51`, "Case Expressions" | none | climb batch 7R rung J's skeptic | The checker's rule tests `ancestors(tt) + tt` for `Contains` only for a `TraitType` and answers false otherwise (`Functionals.scala:1396-1402`, `Types.isContainsType`, `Types.java:145-148`, at c124c6b65). For `varGuard[\R extends Range[\ZZ32\]\](r: R, x: ZZ32) = case x of r => … end` and a guard `(if b then 1:3 else 5#)` it picks `=`, which applies without error, so such a compiled clause never matches. Walk tests the guard's value (`Evaluator.java:416`) and picks ∈; the compiler's world picks ∈ for the type variable. Fix: answer a type variable through its bounds, a union through all members, an intersection through any. Probes `SkCaseGuards*.fss`, gone. full text: history, row 480 |
